    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_8ccd69b0b7a7741162b5efc4e6e0d78b20094b2266acfee0bba5b5bebb619cff",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The Euler product represents unique factorization analytically in the absolutely convergent half-plane. The decisive nonvanishing certificate is not merely that individual Euler factors are nonzero: bounding the reciprocal modulus of the whole product by zeta of the real part, then using the real-axis estimate, yields the positive lower bound |zeta(s)|>(sigma-1)/sigma and hence nonvanishing for sigma>1. Taking absolute values gains positivity and a quantitative certificate but forgets phase and individual-prime behavior.",
